QUESTION 5 : What is the difference between a Local Repository and a Remote Repository?
====================================================================

Interview Ready Answer

A Local Repository is stored on the developer's local machine,
where code is written, committed, and managed. It supports
offline development.

A Remote Repository is hosted on a remote Git server such as
GitHub. It is used for collaboration, code sharing, backup,
and maintaining a central copy of the project.

Definition

Local Repository
• Stored on local machine
• Write code
• Commit code
• Branch management
• Works offline

Remote Repository
• Hosted on GitHub
• Team collaboration
• Code sharing
• Backup
• Pull Requests
• Code Reviews

Important Keywords

• Local Machine
• Remote Git Server
• Collaboration
• Offline Development
• Central Repository

Memory Trick

Local = Work

Remote = Share

====================================================================
INTERVIEW TRAP
====================================================================

Q:
Can you create commits in a Remote Repository?

Answer:
No.

Commits are always created in the Local Repository.
The Remote Repository receives them only after git push.

====================================================================
QUICK REVISION
====================================================================

• Local Repository → Local machine.
• Remote Repository → GitHub/Remote Git server.
• Local = Development.
• Remote = Collaboration.
• Commits happen locally.
• Push/Pull synchronize both repositories.